a heavy-duty hand cannon with an  imposing title and a big bang to match  feels good  sounds good the desert eagle is one of  the most famous handguns in history it's  found in the arsenal of many a movie  villain and video game gunslinger but  it's actually  seldom seen in the real world so how did  a gun that sees almost no serious use  gets so much pop culture attention i've  come to the royal armouries museum in  the uk to find out why the des eagle  might not have been the all-conquering  weapon that games and movies would have  you believe let's lock and load  [Music]  the desert eagle was designed by u.s  company magnum research between 1979 and  1982 with the aim of creating a high  caliber handgun at this point in firearm  history if you were after a sidearm that  packed a punch in the palm of your hand  you were limited to revolvers later  design work was refined by israeli  military industries or imi for short who  also worked on the desert eagles mass  production this combined effort between  manufacturers resulted in the desi eagle  mark 1 with their work allowing for this  new mammoth gas-operated pistol to be  chambered in multiple large calibers  namely the 357 and 44 magnum at the time  of its introduction in 1983 the desert  eagle was unlike any other handgun on  the market and with the introduction of  the even larger caliber of 50 action  express in future models the desert  eagle would further push the limits of  how much power a handgun could unleash  many of you watching this may have seen  this weapon appear in games and other  media under a different name but still  with the iconic .50 adorning its title  from the d50 the nighthawk 50c and the  50gs an iconic part of the weapon is a  nod to its high power and caliber but  when it comes to this handheld 50 cal  what does the term actually mean we have  to differentiate between what the word  calibre actually means  which is the internal diameter of the  barrel of a firearm which obviously has  a direct relationship to the diameter of  the bullet and caliber as a sort of  synonym for cartridge type so 50 action  express that the desert eagle fires is a  cartridge type and it's a lot shorter  than  50 browning machine gun the difference  in size is is immense and two completely  level different levels of destructive  power as a result you might have a  similar mass similar diameter of  projectile but you're not propelling the  thing at anything like the same velocity  to fully appreciate what makes the  desert eagle so unique among handguns  and why it sees such limited real world  use we need to go back to the core of  its design there's a lot that sets the  desert eagle apart its distinctive  silhouette imposing shape often seen  shining in chrome or even an audacious  gold it's like  a  movie star a dirty herald but perhaps  the first thing many would notice is its  sheer size this thing is huge coming in  as standard in 10.75 inches in length  nearly three inches longer than the  standard glock 17. and with that size of  course comes weight the mark 1950 ae  version of the desert eagle weighs just  shy of 2 kilos or 4.4 pounds and that's  when the thing is empty while the size  and weight of the weapon are essential  to facilitate its operation the  distinctive design has become a  shorthand for powerful hand cannons in  many of our video games even within  unique fictional arsenals echoes of its  design or references to its power can be  seen inspiring video game weaponry like  halo's m6 series of magnums the  post-apocalyptic 10 millimeter pistol or  fallout 3 in new vegas or even as the  kramer 0.50 magnum in aliens fire team  elite  its considerable power even demands  respect when the hands holding it are  made from pixels and polygons and the  fact that it's almost always shown  firmly gripped in two hands is another  subtle nod to its sheer power a useful  visual indicator to players that the  sidearm in their possession is about to  throw out some heavy recoil soldiers and  special forces from call of duty  battlefield far cry and counter-strike  all model the weapon with this in mind  and even the larger-than-life characters  like sirius sam or max payne pay the  desert eagle due respect when it comes  to using their side up although that  wouldn't stop old max from flying  through the air duel wielding them in  both single and multiplayer games it's  frequently one of the last guns to  unlock or one of the most expensive ones  to purchase further creating an aura of  mystique or rarity to what is already  considered a skill cannon in most video  games but in spite of the high price for  entry or the long grind the desi eagle  often takes to unlock the reward isn't  always worth the effort commonly having  a slow rate of fire limited range and a  small magazine to balance its power the  desert eagle is often outside of a few  exceptions seldom seen in our  multiplayer battlefields however that  aspect is much closer to its real use  than you may first think despite its  reputation and massive pop culture  footprint that sees it featuring in over  600 films tv shows and video games the  desert eagle is actually used by well  almost no one officially we know that  the polish special forces grom have them  listed in their inventory of weapons and  the portuguese special operations group  have some access to the 357 versions of  the pistol but it's unclear whether  within these organizations the desert  eagles are for familiarization purposes  or as an actually issued sidearm so the  pistol's real world use may actually be  even more rare than we earlier assumed  so despite being the most powerful  handgun in the world it's not a power  that any organization or force in the  real world seems to be that interested  in harnessing i get asked quite a bit  whether there is any practical purpose  for something like a desert eagle the  answer is from a military and law  enforcement arguably even a self-defense  perspective there is no  real practical purpose for it it was  designed to be the biggest and best  in its class  and it it pulled that off there are  semi-practical uses for it certain parts  of the world from for example the united  states have hunting seasons so that  means there are handgun hunting seasons  and now obviously the most powerful  handgun you can lay your hands on  is desirable for hunting self-defense  against very large animals something  that can deliver an awful lot of energy  and stop the thing before it claws your  face off you can see how there might be  a use for that and the final actual real  world use is in target shooting even  then it's it's pretty niche within a  niche so in theory hunting target  shooting and really there it's about the  challenge of controlling the recoil of a  magnum handgun it's easy to see why it  hasn't caught on with the likes of  police military or special forces though  it is undoubtedly incredibly powerful  pure power isn't necessarily the most  appealing prospect for many tactical  situations even loaded with 357 the  desert eagle has less than half the  capacity of regular handguns not to  mention it's impractically big and the  concept of carrying that ridiculous  weight is no doubt an ugly one for any  soldier already weighed down with kits  and equipment not only that but it's  pricey the gun itself can sometimes cost  in excess of two thousand dollars and  can cost two to three dollars per round  pre-tax for 50 ae so it's loud flashy  powerful and expensive to put it simply  they only built it because they could as  with many larger-than-life firearms the  legacy of the desert eagle mostly rests  on the shoulders of its pop culture  impact  according to an article from the  baltimore sun magnum research were  actively soliciting prop houses to use  their gun in their films of course  weapons appearing in movies has always  been an avenue for manufacturers to  advertise their wares and grow their  brand but to me this shows that the  people behind the desert eagle were  always well aware of its image and  potential on the big screen and were  more than happy to allow it to become a  celebrity gun its appearance in the  hands of a huge spectrum of characters  means it has become an iconic weapon for  some of the most imposing characters but  by and large the personalities that have  taken the sidearm onto the silver screen  have given the desert eagle an air of  being a bad guy gun it's the weapon of  choice for bullet dodging agents in the  matrix movies including the latest  matrix resurrections and one of its most  famous video game appearances is pointed  directly down the camera towards the  player by khalid al-assad in call of  duty 4 modern warfare  it is flashy and overkill in more than  one sense meaning it also sits right at  home in the arsenals of the cockiest and  most showboating of characters be that  the mercenary sam serious stone or seen  in the hands of lo wang in the high  octane shadow warrior  in the hands of the traditional good guy  though the desert eagle is usually  reserved for the action hero whose  stature suits their large silver sidearm  whether that is john matrix in the  spectacular 1985 action movie commando  us marshal john kruger in the 1996  action thriller eraser or in the action  fantasy cult classic film within a film  last action hero wielded by the  formidable jack slater  there is something similar about these  guys though i've spoken to a number of  prop masters and armorers over the years  you have to pick something of a suitable  size and presence for the character so  that's in terms of their their  personality you know are they the big  action hero like arnie maybe they're the  villain like you know bullet tooth tony  desert eagle fits there as well  something like the desert eagle has so  much presence it's so physically large  that it still looks like a big gun even  though it's being held by a big guy how  is that guy  conor frase moving away from silver  screens and onto smaller ones one of its  most famous video game appearances is  also responsible for one of its most  common nicknames the deagle in 1999  counter-strike was born as a mod for the  game half-life and would make a full  release a year later after valve  acquired the team and the intellectual  property for those of you who are  unfamiliar with counter-strike it pits  two teams against each other in the  defending and taking of objectives like  hostages or bomb sites the pistol class  of course featuring the desert eagle  named in-game as the nighthawk .50c  although in all the entries of  counter-strike that i've played be that  the original cs source or global  offensive the deagle is the only name  you'll ever hear originally this pistol  was known by its full name but was  changed before counter-strike would  leave its beta branches but because of  this the files retained references to  their original names and titles and no  doubt to keep things nice and neat the  programmers simply shorten the file  names in the case of this pistol d eagle  but despite the name change players knew  exactly what this thing was and since cs  is a game about communication and  call-outs desert eagle doesn't exactly  roll off the tongue so the portmanteau  of deagle became the de facto name fans  gave to counter-strike's hand cannon  across the franchise and that nickname  is still used today the deagle already  had its share of fame from its time in  cinema but the success of counter-strike  meant a spotlight was cast on the game  and the weapon's virtual adaptation also  got a share of that attention from the  early 2000s the desert eagle became a  popular choice to diversify the  selection of sidearms in virtual  shooters it was a part of launching many  franchises that are still popular today  appearing in the first ever far cry game  the first max payne hitman codename 47  the first in that franchise the first  tomb raider featured a pair of 50 cal  pistols seen to be desi eagles in the  more graphically detailed anniversary  edition of the game and of course as  previously mentioned in call of duty 4  modern warfare anytime you need a weapon  that's big scary looking all those all  those sharp edges be that agents in the  matrix or the action hero it's just got  more presence compare it to  conan the barbarian sword your hero  sword has to be big and impressive it's  the same with the handgun and there  aren't that many giant handguns around  you either have to give them a big old  revolver or a deagle the desert eagle is  an iconic firearm whose life in popular  culture and reality are almost at polar  opposites the impact it's had on video  games movies tv shows and larger than  life action stories far outweighs its  real world use  which is probably for the best in movies  it's a weapon to add a sense of power  and intensity to a hero or villain in  games it's a sidearm to show off skill  or to diversify a player's weapon wheel  and in reality it's a team of engineers  pushing boundaries and a firearm for  enthusiasts range shooters and hunters  it completely falls short of the needs  of real world professional soldiers and  organizations but it's shown in those  exact situations and arsenals in our  fiction where pesky things like physics  weight recoil or logistics just don't  matter and that secures the fact that  the desert eagle's legacy is one that  has found a home in our imagination and  in our entertainment i do so love a good  firearm in my hand  i hope you enjoyed this episode of  loadout if you did hit the like button  subscribe for more like this and let me  know your thoughts either in the comment  section down below or reach out to me on  twitter at a regular day  let us know what you'd like to see  future episodes cover and if you want  more video game firearms content you can  watch previous episodes of the show or  check out our weekly series of firearms  expert reacts where i show our friends  the royal armories a selection of guns  from our favorite games until next time  i've been your host dave dewitt thank  you again for watching and i'll see you  in the next episode  [Music]  you

